-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction Policy
+
+The cache uses a 2Q (two-queue) eviction strategy. Entries are classified into two populations: **in** (one-time accesses) and **out** (reused entries). New cache hits enter the **in** queue. On a subsequent access within an aging window, the entry promotes to the **out** queue, where it remains longer. When the cache must evict, entries leave the **in** queue first, protecting sheets that have proven reusable during long screenings where many one-use sheets accumulate. The size split is 75/25: the **in** queue holds up to 75% of the budget, and the **out** queue holds up to 25%. Within each queue, eviction is FIFO.
+
+This approach tolerates both sequential screenings (where each sheet is rendered once and forgotten) and comparison workflows (where editors revisit a stable working set). Unlike LRU, it does not thrash when scanning past an archive of one-use clips; unlike aged frequency counting, it does not require counter maintenance or risk stale aging windows.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (two-queue with promotion on reuse) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Long screenings produce many one-use sheets that would evict useful comparison clips under LRU. Aged frequency is simpler but aging window tuning and counter maintenance add operational overhead and risk lag when editors switch projects. 2Q separates accidental clutter from intentional reuse with minimal bookkeeping: only one promotion event per clip, no aging window tuning, and queue-size split (75/25) is stable across workloads. |
